# PHP: cannot declare class because the name is already in use

When you attempt to include `somescript.php` which loads both `child1.php` and `child2.php`, PHP throws a fatal error regarding the `Database` class. The specific message indicates that the class name is already in use within `database.php`. While loading just one child script works perfectly, combining them breaks the application because of how PHP handles file inclusion and global state.

The root cause lies in the execution order and the presence of duplicate definitions. When `somescript.php` runs, it likely includes or requires both children. If `child1.php` is processed first, it executes its `include 'parent.php'` statement. This triggers the loading of `database.php`, defining the `Database` class for the first time. Subsequently, when `child2.php` is included, it also runs `include 'parent.php'`. Since `parent.php` contains `include 'database.php'`, PHP attempts to define the `Database` class again in the same global scope. In PHP, classes are singletons within a process; you cannot redefine them unless specific conditions are met. This results in the "cannot declare class" fatal error.

To resolve this, you must ensure that `database.php` is included only once per request lifecycle, regardless of how many times it is requested by child classes. The standard solution involves using an autoloader or a dedicated initialization file to manage dependencies centrally.

Instead of having every script include `database.php` directly, create a central bootstrap file, such as `app/bootstrap.php`. This file should load the database definition and then provide a method for other files to initialize their own classes. You can structure `parent.php` to check if the parent class already exists before defining it:

```php
// app/bootstrap.php
require_once __DIR__ . '/database.php';

if (!class_exists('Parent')) {
    // Define Parent here only if it hasn't been loaded yet
    // Or better yet, move the Parent definition to a separate file
}
```

A more robust approach is to decouple the class definitions from the inclusion logic. Move the `Database` class into its own namespace or use an autoloader like Composer. With Composer, you define the dependencies in your `composer.json`, and the autoloader ensures that `database.php` is loaded exactly once when any of its classes are instantiated. This prevents duplicate includes entirely.

Alternatively, if you must stick to manual includes without Composer, implement a simple check in `parent.php` or a wrapper function. However, simply adding `if (!class_exists('Database'))` inside `database.php` is not the right pattern for fixing the include chain; that file should define the class unconditionally. The fix belongs in how you orchestrate the includes.

Consider refactoring `somescript.php` to load dependencies explicitly once:

```php
// somescript.php
require_once 'app/database.php'; // Load database once at the top

include 'child1.php';
include 'child2.php';

$child1 = new Child1();
$child2 = new Child2();
```

By moving the `require_once 'database.php'` to the very beginning of your entry point, you guarantee that the `Database` class is defined in memory before any child scripts attempt to load it. When `child1.php` and `child2.php` subsequently include `parent.php`, the check inside `parent.php` (or simply the fact that the class already exists) prevents the duplicate definition error.

Another valid pattern is to ensure that `parent.php` does not blindly include `database.php`. Instead, pass the database instance or let the child scripts handle the dependency injection if they are using a framework like Laravel. In modern PHP development, relying on frameworks for dependency management is often preferred over manual file inclusion chains. For instance, integrating with [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) resources can provide insights into how Laravel handles service providers and facades to avoid such namespace collisions automatically.

Ultimately, the goal is to treat class definitions as singletons that should be instantiated or checked for existence before re-definition. By centralizing the inclusion of `database.php` in your entry script or utilizing an autoloader, you eliminate the ambiguity of duplicate includes and restore stability to your application flow.
